  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We are to improve the upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The current record is about 0.3808. We'll use a two-stage approach: first optimize a non-integer representation of the function, then a simpler binary representation. This allows balancing between exploration (fuzzy transitions) and exploitation (sharp discontinuities). We maintain two parallel searches to avoid local optima traps. The approach leverages smooth optimization to escape binary local minima while using binary annealing to explore rugged solution spaces. We'll monitor worst-case shift performance dynamically and adjust optimization focus accordingly.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^(n_points) with \u2211h_i * dx = 1 (dx=2/n_points)\n   Objective: Minimize max_k (np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full') * (2/n_points))\n   Representation:\n     Stage 1: Non-integer (fuzzy) representation\n     Stage 2: Binary representation (h_i \u2208 {0,1})\n   Optimization:\n     Stage 1: SLSQP optimization of a soft-max objective\n       Objective: (1/\u03b2) * log(\u2211 exp(\u03b2*(1 - overlap_\u03c9))) for some selected shift \u03c9\n     Stage 2: Binary simulated annealing with local improvement\n   Constraints:\n     Sum(h_i) * dx = 1 exactly for all representations\n     Individual h_i in [0,1] (for fuzzy) or {0,1} (binary)\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a) Input: n_points (even) from {360,480,600,720}\n   b) Initial generation:\n        Start with interpolation/upsampling of parent if available\n        For fuzzy: log-normal initialization projected to sum constraint\n        For binary: minimum binary representation with n_points/2 ones\n   c) Stage 1 (fuzzy optimization):\n        \u2022 SLSQP on soft-max of cross-correlation\n        \u2022 Parameters: \u03b2 \u2208 [100,5e3], max_iter=500\n        \u2022 At each step: clip to [0,1] and adjust sum exactly\n        \u2022 Record score C5 = max_k cross-correlation * (2/n_points)\n   d) Transition to binary:\n        \u2022 Threshold fuzzy solution to binary\n        \u2022 Convert to minimum binary representation (ones and zeros with \u2211=n_points/2)\n   e) Stage 2 (binary optimization):\n        \u2022 Binary simulated annealing with:\n            - Temperature schedule based on current C5\n            - Acceptance prob: exp(-\u0394C5/T)\n            - Local improvement: greedy swaps fixing worst shift\n        \u2022 Removed elements replaced based on shift-time filtering\n   f) Final cleanup:\n        \u2022 Greedy improvement attempts\n        \u2022 Sum adjustment post-binary transformation\n   g) Output: Best h_values from all representations\n\n4. Search choices:\n   a) Stage 1: \n      - Initialization: log-normal with mean=0.5 for all points initially\n      - \u03b2 schedule: descend from 100 to 5000 in powers of 10\n      - Population size: 4 independent runs\n   b) Stage 2:\n      - Exponential cooling schedule with multistarts\n      - Randomized swapping strategy with priority to high overlap positions\n   c) Evaluation strategy:\n      - Track median C5 across 4 stage1 runs separately\n      - If fuzzy gets C5 \u2265 0.3808, switch to binary immediately\n      - Otherwise maximum iterations=1500 seconds\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n   - Time allocation: 80% Stage 1, 20% Stage 2 (but adaptive to C5 improvement)\n   - Dimension selection: {360,480,600,720} in descending order\n   - Sum adjustment: adjust_sum_exact function preserving constraint\n   - Recovery: binary representation from fuzzy final solution\n   - Dimension change: only if \u221aC5 improves significantly\n\n6. Validation:\n   - Final verify: 0<=h_i<=1, sum(h)\u2248n_points/2\n   - Recompute C5 exactly: max_k np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')*(2/n_points)\n   - If C5 \u2265 0.3807, archive solution",
